Review and test the SEP-based rewrite of astrotilt. The goal is to determine whether it is fit for measuring how close astrophotography stars are to round, while preserving the current CLI behavior and 3×3 cell summary output.
Please focus on these tests:
- Verify the CLI still behaves the same way, especially astrotilt --verbose ., directory/file handling, and the existing median/mean/std 3×3 output.
- Confirm the SEP implementation is correct:
  - sep.Background is used appropriately.
  - background is subtracted before shape measurement.
  - sep.extract thresholding really corresponds to the intended sigma threshold.
  - a, b, and theta are interpreted correctly.
  - eccentricity is computed as sqrt(1 - (b/a)^2).
  - stars are detected on the whole frame and only afterward assigned to grid cells by centroid.
  - SEP flags, invalid shapes, edge detections, blends, saturated objects, etc. are handled sensibly.
- Compare the SEP rewrite against the original astrotilt on exactly the same FITS files. In particular, use representative 10 s and 20 s NINA subs if available. Report:
  - star counts
  - 3×3 mean eccentricity
  - 3×3 median eccentricity
  - frame-to-frame standard deviation
  - any systematic differences between old and new versions.
- Test absolute eccentricity accuracy with synthetic stars. Generate noise-added circular and elliptical Gaussian or Moffat stars with known axis ratios. Include several S/N levels and subpixel centroid positions. Check whether:
  - circular stars give eccentricity close to 0
  - known elliptical stars recover the expected value
  - results are stable with changing brightness/background
  - results are stable when a star falls near a 3×3 grid boundary.
- Test difficult real-world cases:
  - close/blended stars
  - saturated stars
  - stars near frame edges
  - nebulous or spatially varying backgrounds
  - low-S/N stars
  - crowded fields.
- Determine whether the current filtering is adequate for an absolute “round-star” metric. If not, recommend minimal additional cuts, preferably using SEP-native information such as flags, flux/SNR, npix, peak, a/b, or saturation thresholds.
- Check whether median eccentricity is more stable than mean eccentricity and whether the program should make median the primary metric.
- Add or evaluate orientation (theta) reporting. The eventual goal is not just to say “stars are elongated,” but to distinguish:
  - common orientation across field → tracking/guiding/vibration
  - radial/tangential pattern → backfocus / optical aberration
  - spatial gradient → tilt/decentering.
The main acceptance question is: Can this SEP-based version reliably answer “are my stars close to round?” and “is one part of the field systematically worse than another?”
Do not over-engineer it yet. Prefer validating the simple SEP approach before proposing PSF fitting or a larger redesign.

The most valuable result from the agent would be a small table showing true synthetic eccentricity vs measured SEP eccentricity at several S/N levels. That will tell us whether a threshold such as e < 0.5 actually has a defensible meaning.
